WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.020
In this video, you build a generalized Windows image in SUSE Virtualization: an answer file installs and syspreps a build machine, you export its disk, and deploy a new machine from it.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:17.800
Start with a build virtual machine. On the create page, choose the demo namespace,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.340
and name the machine demo-win-build.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:26.160
Select Use the virtual machine template to start from a ready layout.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:36.200
Choose windows-iso-medium-template. It brings the installer CD-ROM, the VMDP driver CD, a root disk and the Hyper-V settings.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.870
Lower the memory to eight gigabytes, which is enough for the build.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:47.070
Set OS Type to Windows. This adds the Windows Unattended and Sysprep section to Advanced Options.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.760
Open Advanced Options and scroll to the Windows sysprep section.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:59.540
Select demo-unattend. It holds the self-contained Server 2025 answer file from the harvester-windows-cloudbase repository, unchanged.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:10.010
The answer file installs Windows, then a first-logon script adds the VMDP drivers and Cloudbase-Init, cleans up, and runs sysprep.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.210
On the Volumes tab, set the installer image and the root disk size.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:21.530
On this release, the template's CD-ROM slot doesn't keep the ISO's storage class, so remove this placeholder CD-ROM

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.290
and add the installer as a new image volume instead.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.830
Set its type to CD-ROM,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:34.130
then search the Image list for the Windows Server 2025 evaluation ISO

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.810
and select it. It was uploaded under Images earlier.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:44.080
Move the new CD-ROM up with its arrow button, so the machine boots the installer first.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.890
One more move up, and the installer is the first boot device again.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:56.530
Shrink the root disk to thirty-six gigabytes. The image keeps this size, and drive C grows on every deployed copy.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.910
Select Create, and the build runs on its own from here, without any clicks.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:10.330
demo-win-build is running. Windows Setup reads the answer file from a small CD that the cluster attached.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.930
To watch the install, open the Console menu of demo-win-build

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:20.860
and select Open in WebVNC to see the Windows screen.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:31.880
Windows Setup installs the operating system with no questions on screen, because the answer file already answers them.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:40.650
After about twenty minutes, sysprep generalized the system and shut it down. The machine is off, and its disk is the golden image.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.730
On the Volumes page, open the action menu of the build machine's root disk.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:50.210
Select Export Image to copy this disk into a new image.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.700
In the dialog, choose the demo namespace for the new image,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.160
and name the image demo-win-golden, short and without spaces.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:05.250
Set the storage class explicitly to lvm-thin, or the image lands on the default class.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.950
Then select Create to start copying the disk into the image.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:16.660
The image is ready. Now create a virtual machine from it, again in the demo namespace,

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.210
and name it demo-win-web. This name becomes the Windows hostname.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.200
Use a virtual machine template again,

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:31.950
this time windows-image-template, made for prepared Windows images. It needs no installer ISO.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:35.620
Lower the memory to eight gigabytes for this test server.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.850
On the Volumes tab, the root disk from the template has no image yet.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.360
Remove this placeholder disk,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.670
and add the image as a new disk instead. Select Add a Virtual Machine Image.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:52.370
In the Image list, search for demo-win-golden, the image you just exported,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.310
and select it for the new root disk.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:01.930
In Advanced Options, the template's cloud-config is already filled in. Cloudbase-Init reads it on the first boot.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:08.800
It sets the time zone to UTC. Cloudbase-Init takes the hostname from the virtual machine name, and grows drive C to the disk.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:16.540
Select Create. The cluster clones the image into the new root disk and starts the machine.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:26.130
demo-win-web is running. Windows finishes setup on its own, and Cloudbase-Init applies the hostname and the cloud-config.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.200
When setup is done, open the Console menu of demo-win-web

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.970
and select Open in WebVNC. No setup screens are waiting, only the lock screen.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.530
Send Control Alt Delete from the toolbar to open the sign-in,

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:46.830
and confirm it in the dialog.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:53.060
The generalized image has no Administrator password yet, so Windows asks you to set one at the first sign-in.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:59.140
Press Enter to accept, and Windows shows the new password fields.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.170
Type a new password,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.690
move to the confirmation field,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:09.630
and type it again, then press Enter.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:15.550
Windows confirms that the password changed. Press Enter to sign in.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:20.820
You are on the desktop. Open the Run dialog with Windows plus R.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.880
Run a command prompt that prints the computer name.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:34.520
The computer name is demo-win-web, the name you gave the virtual machine. Setup finished without any prompts.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:45.277
The new virtual machine came from the generalized image and finished setup on its own, with the hostname from its name. Next, you resize virtual machines through GitOps.
